  "Question": "If \\(xy=a,\\) \\(xz=b,\\) and \\(yz=c,\\) and none of these quantities is \\(0,\\) then \\(x^2+y^2+z^2\\) equals",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\frac{ab+ac+bc}{abc}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\frac{a^2+b^2+c^2}{abc}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\frac{(a+b+c)^2}{abc}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\frac{(ab+ac+bc)^2}{abc}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\frac{(ab)^2+(ac)^2+(bc)^2}{abc}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Multiplying two of the given products and dividing by the third cancels two variables and leaves a square. Because \\(a,b,c\\) are nonzero, \\(x,y,z\\) are also nonzero, so these divisions are valid:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2&=\\frac{(xy)(xz)}{yz}=\\frac{ab}{c},\\\\\ny^2&=\\frac{(xy)(yz)}{xz}=\\frac{ac}{b},\\\\\nz^2&=\\frac{(xz)(yz)}{xy}=\\frac{bc}{a}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nNow use the nonzero common denominator \\(abc\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2+y^2+z^2\n&=\\frac{ab}{c}+\\frac{ac}{b}+\\frac{bc}{a}\\\\\n&=\\frac{a^2b^2+a^2c^2+b^2c^2}{abc}\\\\\n&=\\frac{(ab)^2+(ac)^2+(bc)^2}{abc}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThis is choice E.",
